"""
# Imported Generic HEAD + ARGUMENT Projection Resolve in the OWNER's Scope

Follow-up (e) of `hir_unresolved_type_owner_missing_import_2026-08-22.md` and the
re-land condition of `hir_generic_projection_regression_run15_2026-08-22.md`.

## The defect, in the shape this fixture builds

`imported_surface_type` projects an imported callable's signature through the
OWNER's qualified scope. Its scalar arm reads
`parser_type_kind_named_name(type_.kind)`, which for `Dict<text, Payload>`
answers **`Dict`** -- the OUTER generic constructor, not the argument. `Dict`,
`Option` and `Result` are BUILTIN generics: nothing ever calls
`bind_qualified_type` for them, so `lookup_qualified_type_raw(owner, "Dict")`
misses **every time**. The arm then fell through to `self.lower_type(type_)`,
which re-lowers the WHOLE type in the **IMPORTER's** scope and recurses into the
generic ARGUMENT there. `Payload` -- which the importer never imported and never
names -- is what hard-errors, with a synthesized span blaming the importer.

Measured on the real tree by the `[ist-scalar-fallthrough]` probe:

    [ist-scalar-fallthrough] name=Dict owner=compiler.mono.monomorphize_integration
                             lowering=src/compiler/mono/instantiation.spl

Note the name is `Dict`. That is why every OWNER-side repair failed: 75
owner-side imports across `40.mono` left the fatal count completely unchanged,
because once the type reaches `lower_type` in the importer's scope what the owner
imports is irrelevant.

## Why the previous two attempts did not settle it

* `d481f15e1ac` recursed into the ARGUMENTS but left the HEAD falling through, so
  the importer-scope re-lowering still happened. Stage-1 went 48 -> 3716 fatals /
  437 poisoned modules. Reverted at `ec13c319250`.
* The predecessor fixture
  `imported_optional_argument_reexport_hop_regression_spec.spl` is GREEN on both
  sides of that regression -- a four-module `export use` facade hop does not
  reach the miss. It is kept, labelled a non-reproducer in its own docstring.

**This fixture discriminates**, and the discrimination was verified by running it
against the pristine tree and against the fixed tree rather than assumed. The
shape that makes it work is not the facade depth -- it is that the OUTER
constructor is a builtin generic, which misses the qualified lookup
unconditionally, so no amount of import plumbing in the owner can mask it.

## The fix this pins

Project a generic HEAD-AND-ARGUMENTS: recurse each argument through
`imported_surface_type` in the OWNER's scope (which is exactly where
`materialize_imported_callable_dependency` bound it, via
`bind_qualified_type(imported_mod.module_name, dep, symbol)`), then hand those
ALREADY-LOWERED `HirType`s to `lower_named_kind`, which resolves the head by name
and has real arms for `Option`/`Result`/`Dict`. `lower_type` is never reached for
a generic, so the importer's scope is never consulted for an argument.

This restores the symmetry that is the root theme of the parent record: the
MATERIALIZATION walk already recurses Array -> Tuple -> Named and binds every
argument correctly; only PROJECTION failed to consult what it bound.

## The silent drop is NOT an acceptable alternative

`case 5` pins the argument identity, not merely the absence of an error. Before
the generic-argument lane the argument was DROPPED (`Named(id, [])`), which made
the miss unreachable at the cost of silently erasing `Dict<text, MirType>` to
`Dict<any, any>` in the importer's symbol table with ZERO diagnostics. A future
change that "fixes" a regression by restoring the drop must fail this spec.
"""

use compiler.hir.hir_lowering.types.{HirLowering, hirlowering_for_module}
use compiler.hir.hir_lowering.items.*
use compiler.hir.hir_types.{HirType, HirTypeKind, SymbolKind}
use compiler.hir.hir_lowering.module_surface.{ModuleSurfacesByName, module_surfaces_from_modules}
use compiler.frontend.frontend.{parse_full_frontend}
use compiler.frontend.parser_types.{Module}
use compiler.common.driver_core_types.{SourceFile}
use compiler.common.config.{Logger}

fn head_arg_surfaces(mods: Dict<text, Module>, srcs: [SourceFile]) -> ModuleSurfacesByName:
    match module_surfaces_from_modules(mods, srcs):
        case Ok(value): value
        case Err(error):
            assert_true(false)
            ModuleSurfacesByName.empty()

fn head_arg_lower(owner_decl: text) -> HirLowering:
    """Three modules, the minimal shape that reaches the defect.

    `decl.payload_types` DECLARES `Payload`.
    `svc.provider` explicitly imports it and exposes one callable naming it
    inside a generic constructor.
    `svc.consumer` imports ONLY the callable -- it never names `Payload`, which
    is precisely the innocent-importer position the parent record describes.
    """
    val log = Logger(level: 0)
    val decl_src = "struct Payload:\n    tag: text\n"
    val prov_src = "use decl.payload_types.\{Payload\}\n\n" + owner_decl
    val cons_src = "use svc.provider.\{provide\}\n\nfn go() -> i64:\n    1"
    var mods: Dict<text, Module> = {}
    mods["decl.payload_types"] = parse_full_frontend(decl_src, "decl.payload_types", "decl.payload_types", log)
    mods["svc.provider"] = parse_full_frontend(prov_src, "svc.provider", "svc.provider", log)
    val cons = parse_full_frontend(cons_src, "svc.consumer", "svc.consumer", log)
    mods["svc.consumer"] = cons
    val srcs = [
        SourceFile(path: "decl/payload_types.spl", content: decl_src, module_name: "decl.payload_types"),
        SourceFile(path: "svc/provider.spl", content: prov_src, module_name: "svc.provider"),
        SourceFile(path: "svc/consumer.spl", content: cons_src, module_name: "svc.consumer")
    ]
    var lowering = hirlowering_for_module("svc.consumer", head_arg_surfaces(mods, srcs))
    val hir = lowering.lower_module(cons)
    lowering

fn head_arg_unresolved_payload(lowering: HirLowering) -> i64:
    """Count of `unresolved type: Payload` blamed on the consumer.

    This is the exact diagnostic the parent record tracks as `[hir-fatal]`.
    """
    var count = 0
    for error in lowering.errors:
        if error.message.contains("unresolved type: Payload") or
           error.message.contains("unresolved name: Payload"):
            eprint("[head-arg-unresolved] {error.message}")
            count = count + 1
    count

fn head_arg_dict_value_shape(lowering: HirLowering) -> text:
    """Shape of the VALUE argument of `provide`'s projected `Dict` return.

    Returns a descriptor rather than a HirType so the assertion names the
    failure: `erased-any` is the silent defect, `named` is correct.
    """
    val sym_id = lowering.symbols.lookup_or_invalid("provide")
    if not sym_id.is_valid():
        return "no-symbol"
    if not lowering.symbols.symbols.has(sym_id.id):
        return "no-entry"
    val sym = lowering.symbols.symbols[sym_id.id]
    if val t = sym.type_:
        match t.kind:
            case HirTypeKind.Function(params, ret, effects):
                match ret.kind:
                    case HirTypeKind.Dict(key, value):
                        match value.kind:
                            case HirTypeKind.Named(symbol, args): "named"
                            case HirTypeKind.Any: "erased-any"
                            case _: "erased-other"
                    case _: "not-dict"
            case _: "not-function"
    else:
        "no-type"

val DICT_RETURN: text =
    "fn provide(d: Dict<text, Payload>) -> Dict<text, Payload>:\n    d\n"
val OPTIONAL_RETURN: text =
    "fn provide() -> Payload?:\n    nil\n"
val RESULT_RETURN: text =
    "fn provide(p: Payload) -> Result<Payload, text>:\n    Ok(p)\n"
val PLAIN_RETURN: text =
    "fn provide(p: Payload) -> Payload:\n    p\n"


fn field_lower(field_decl: text) -> HirLowering:
    """The FIELD twin of `head_arg_lower`.

    `verification_contract: HirContractBlock?` -- an OPTIONAL FIELD of an
    imported composite -- is the single largest run15 population (501 fatals),
    and it runs through a DIFFERENT materialization site than callable params
    (`module_import_registration.spl`, not `module_reexport_materialization.spl`).
    Both sites had the identical scalar-head gap, so the field path needs its
    own case; a callable-only fixture would have passed while fields flooded.
    """
    val log = Logger(level: 0)
    val decl_src = "struct Payload:\n    tag: text\n"
    val prov_src = "use decl.payload_types.\{Payload\}\n\n" + field_decl
    val cons_src = "use svc.provider.\{Holder\}\n\nfn go() -> i64:\n    1"
    var mods: Dict<text, Module> = {}
    mods["decl.payload_types"] = parse_full_frontend(decl_src, "decl.payload_types", "decl.payload_types", log)
    mods["svc.provider"] = parse_full_frontend(prov_src, "svc.provider", "svc.provider", log)
    val cons = parse_full_frontend(cons_src, "svc.consumer", "svc.consumer", log)
    mods["svc.consumer"] = cons
    val srcs = [
        SourceFile(path: "decl/payload_types.spl", content: decl_src, module_name: "decl.payload_types"),
        SourceFile(path: "svc/provider.spl", content: prov_src, module_name: "svc.provider"),
        SourceFile(path: "svc/consumer.spl", content: cons_src, module_name: "svc.consumer")
    ]
    var lowering = hirlowering_for_module("svc.consumer", head_arg_surfaces(mods, srcs))
    val hir = lowering.lower_module(cons)
    lowering

val OPTIONAL_FIELD: text =
    "struct Holder:\n    slot: Payload?\n"
val DICT_FIELD: text =
    "struct Holder:\n    slots: Dict<text, Payload>\n"

describe "imported generic head and arguments resolve in the owner's scope":
    it "does not blame the consumer for a Dict VALUE argument it never names":
        # GUARD, not the reproducer -- green on both sides here, and said so
        # rather than left to imply otherwise. In THIS fixture the head miss
        # routes through `imported_surface_type_projected`, which drops the
        # arguments SILENTLY, so no error is raised pre-fix and this case
        # cannot discriminate. The discriminating case is the erasure one
        # below.
        #
        # Its value is as a REGRESSION guard on the repair: projecting
        # arguments WITHOUT the materialization half makes exactly this case
        # fail with `unresolved type: Payload` -- the `d481f15e1ac` flood shape,
        # measured here in seconds instead of a 72-minute build.
        expect(head_arg_unresolved_payload(head_arg_lower(DICT_RETURN))).to_equal(0)

    it "does not blame the consumer for an OPTIONAL argument (`Payload?`)":
        # `X?` reaches HIR as `Named("Option", [X])`, so the head is the builtin
        # `Option` and the mechanism is identical. This is the shape that carried
        # the largest run15 population (HirContractBlock 501, SymbolId 444).
        expect(head_arg_unresolved_payload(head_arg_lower(OPTIONAL_RETURN))).to_equal(0)

    it "does not blame the consumer for a Result OK argument":
        expect(head_arg_unresolved_payload(head_arg_lower(RESULT_RETURN))).to_equal(0)

    it "still projects the non-generic sibling that already worked":
        # CONTROL. A bare `Payload` head is looked up in the owner's scope and
        # HITS, so this passed before the fix too. It proves the defect is the
        # GENERIC head, not the three-module shape itself -- without it a broken
        # fixture that errors on everything would look like a reproducer.
        expect(head_arg_unresolved_payload(head_arg_lower(PLAIN_RETURN))).to_equal(0)

    it "keeps the Dict VALUE argument resolved instead of erasing it to Any":
        # THE DISCRIMINATING ASSERTION -- verified RED pre-fix, GREEN post-fix.
        #
        # Both projection exits for a scalar head discarded the generic
        # arguments, so `Dict<text, Payload>` reached the importer's symbol
        # table as `Dict<any, any>` with ZERO diagnostics: no error, no probe
        # line, nothing. That is why the four error-count cases above pass on
        # BOTH sides here and cannot be the reproducer -- this fixture routes
        # through `imported_surface_type_projected`, whose miss path
        # (`[ist-proj-miss] name=Dict owner=svc.provider`, observed) drops the
        # arguments silently rather than falling through to `lower_type`.
        #
        # A future change that "fixes" a regression by restoring the drop must
        # fail HERE. That is this case's entire purpose.
        expect(head_arg_dict_value_shape(head_arg_lower(DICT_RETURN))).to_equal("named")

    it "reports no lowering error at all for any generic shape":
        expect(head_arg_lower(DICT_RETURN).errors.len()).to_equal(0)
        expect(head_arg_lower(OPTIONAL_RETURN).errors.len()).to_equal(0)
        expect(head_arg_lower(RESULT_RETURN).errors.len()).to_equal(0)

    it "does not blame the consumer for an OPTIONAL FIELD argument":
        # THE LARGEST run15 POPULATION, and a SEPARATE materialization site from
        # every case above (`module_import_registration.spl`). Without the field
        # half of the fix, projecting field arguments in the owner's scope
        # reproduces the flood here even with the callable half landed.
        expect(head_arg_unresolved_payload(field_lower(OPTIONAL_FIELD))).to_equal(0)

    it "does not blame the consumer for a Dict FIELD argument":
        expect(head_arg_unresolved_payload(field_lower(DICT_FIELD))).to_equal(0)

    it "reports no lowering error for either generic FIELD shape":
        expect(field_lower(OPTIONAL_FIELD).errors.len()).to_equal(0)
        expect(field_lower(DICT_FIELD).errors.len()).to_equal(0)
